WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.840
 - Hello and welcome to chapter six.

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:03.840
 In this chapter, we're going to talk about strings.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.160
 And chapter seven is the payoff chapter.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:08.420
 So we, you know, up to this point,

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:11.200
 we're still learning sort of basic building blocks

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:14.300
 and actually we're going to write a real program in chapter seven.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:18.440
 So just learn this and the payoffs in chapter seven.

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:20.080
 So we actually have been using strings

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:21.400
 from the very first lecture.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:24.760
 'Cause if you print hello world, well, that's a string.

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:26.400
 And so we've been doing things.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:29.640
 This slide here is all review.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:31.500
 We use plus to concatenate strings.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.200
 We use print to print them out.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.560
 Print's just a function that takes as a parameter

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:38.100
 something, strings, integers, et cetera.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:43.240
 We can put digits in strings, but we can't add to them.

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:44.840
 By now you've figured this out,

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:48.000
 but you can use things like ints to convert the strings

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.360
 to integers and then print things out.

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:52.920
 So, you know, we've been doing this for a while.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:54.920
 We've been talking about strings all along.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:58.480
 Now today, what we're going to do is going to

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:02.200
 just get into strings in more detail.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:07.200
 We're reading, we input data with the input function.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.160
 Input returns us a string.

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:12.280
 And if we want to input a number,

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:14.120
 we have to run some kind of conversion,

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:17.940
 like we have to do on int before we take this data

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:20.520
 that we read from input, you know.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:22.920
 And so there's things that we've got to do

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:26.400
 and we've been doing all these things in programs so far.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:29.320
 But if we look in a little more detail inside strings,

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:32.600
 we can index within strings each character.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:35.320
 So each character has a separate position

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:37.300
 and a separate index.

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:42.300
 And they basically, the letters have positions.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:44.560
 And the positions start at zero.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:47.220
 And the best way I explain this to remember this

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:49.400
 is it's the elevators.

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:52.040
 As we used in one of our examples a long time ago,

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:53.640
 elevators in Europe start at zero,

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:57.220
 and so strings start at zero as well.

00:01:57.220 --> 00:01:59.520
 Turns out in the old days there's some efficiency

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:02.760
 with the notion of lists of things starting with zero.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:04.980
 These days the efficiency isn't the issue.

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:07.680
 But there's a certain elegance starting at zero,

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:09.480
 even though intellectually you might think

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:13.800
 one would be the first character in the string.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.760
 Might make most sense to be sub one,

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:17.160
 but it's not, it's sub zero.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:18.960
 But just remember that.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:22.640
 And so we have this operator called the index operator.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:23.960
 And it's square brackets.

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:28.760
 So you know, fruit is a variable

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:30.640
 that contains the string banana.

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:33.680
 And then fruit sub one is the character

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:34.960
 that's in position one.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:37.240
 Now that actually is the second character.

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:39.320
 I'll keep reminding you until I get tired of reminding you.

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:43.200
 And so that assigns A, the letter A,

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:48.200
 into, I mean, the letter A into the variable letter.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.200
 Of course that's a badly chosen,

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:51.860
 it's either a well chosen variable name

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:53.460
 or a badly chosen variable name.

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:56.120
 And the thing that goes inside this

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:58.280
 can either be a constant or it can be an expression.

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:00.120
 So this is X equals three.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:03.160
 And then fruit sub X minus one, well that means two,

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:05.120
 which is position two, which is an N.

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:07.600
 And so that gives us an N.

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:09.640
 So the index is an operator.

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:11.740
 And you can add this bracket syntax

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:13.880
 to the end of a string variable.

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:18.400
 You can't index beyond the length of the string.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:20.800
 So if I say Zot sub five, well,

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:23.800
 there's only three characters, which means zero, one, two,

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:25.880
 but sub five doesn't work.

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:29.480
 And of course we get a happy little traceback.

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:32.540
 So you have to be careful when you're starting

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:33.680
 to pull stuff out of strings.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:35.320
 Although some of the things allow it,

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:38.400
 some of them don't, and then you'll kind of get used to that.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:41.240
 We can ask how long a string is.

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:43.120
 And so we use the len function,

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:45.200
 and we pass the string variable,

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:47.400
 and we pass it into len as parameter,

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.880
 and len gives us back the length of the string,

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:51.480
 not the position.

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:55.280
 So it's zero through len minus one.

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:58.440
 So it's zero through length minus one.

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:00.900
 So length is just another function

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:03.240
 that we've been doing functions now for a while.

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:06.560
 You pass in a parameter, and then len does some work,

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:09.720
 and out comes six, and that goes back into X,

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:11.720
 because the function has a residual value.

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:14.840
 It just happens to be a built-in function.

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:19.080
 And so somewhere deep inside Python

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:21.920
 there is code that takes this,

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:24.360
 and somebody wrote a loop or looked something up,

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:28.160
 and then returned a return value and sent back a six

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:31.480
 to go into our X variable.

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:34.080
 And so a function is there, and like I said,

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:36.440
 we've been using this for a while.

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:38.680
 Another thing we tend to do is to look through strings

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:41.880
 and look at strings and dig data out of strings.

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:45.320
 Python is excellent for doing sort of these kinds

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:46.440
 of lookups.

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:49.720
 And so we can write a simple loop.

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:54.080
 We can write a for loop that creates some kind

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:57.020
 of iteration variable like index.

00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:59.460
 And given that we know that these positions are zero

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:01.880
 through five, we can set this to be zero.

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:04.520
 And then write a while loop while the iteration variable

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:06.640
 is less than the length of fruit.

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:10.720
 And remember, this is six, so it's going to be zero through five.

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:15.140
 Zero through five are the values we want to generate.

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:17.000
 And then we can look up one at a time,

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:19.540
 pull out fruits of index, so fruits of zero,

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:21.680
 fruits of one, two, three, four, five,

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:24.640
 and then print out the position and the letter,

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:27.360
 index, and then add one to index, and it runs,

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:29.920
 this will run six times, zero through five,

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:33.280
 and out we go to produce this output right here.

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:36.000
 And so that's one way of looping through strings.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:40.320
 That is a basic indeterminate loop,

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:43.080
 but we construct carefully an iteration value,

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:45.840
 construct an iteration value,

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:50.840
 and work our way through that loop data.

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:54.080
 The other way is to use a determinant loop, a for loop.

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:57.680
 And generally when we are able to use a while loop

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:00.200
 or a for loop, all else being equal,

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:02.600
 we generally prefer a for loop.

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:05.740
 And so here we have the for keyword and fruit,

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:09.260
 and it's an in, and so for letter in fruit,

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:11.720
 well that just says letter is our iteration variable,

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:13.780
 and it's going to take on the successive values

00:06:13.780 --> 00:06:15.520
 of each of the characters.

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:17.640
 So this loop is going to run six times,

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:22.180
 and letter is going to be b-a-n-a-n-a, banana.

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:24.000
 I'm always terrified when I make these slides

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:26.320
 that I'm going to misspell banana because somehow

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:30.080
 I always think that there are two n's somewhere.

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:33.320
 I don't know, it's not one of my favorite words to spell.

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:36.320
 I actually didn't choose banana as the constant.

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:39.340
 The author who I borrowed the textbook from,

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:41.920
 Alan Downey and Jeff Elkner, they used banana,

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:43.400
 and so I'm still using banana.

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:45.900
 So some of the jokes in the book aren't my book,

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:50.020
 aren't my jokes, they are the jokes of Jeff and Alan.

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:53.120
 So here are just two equivalent, you know,

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:54.400
 so you can have the while loop,

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:55.880
 they sort of both do the same thing,

00:06:55.880 --> 00:06:59.000
 they both just print the letters out one time through,

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:00.940
 each of these loops runs five times,

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:03.720
 but you can see how the determinant loop,

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:06.000
 the for loop, is a prettier loop,

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:08.420
 unless you truly somehow need to know this number

00:07:08.420 --> 00:07:09.400
 as you're going through the loop.

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:10.680
 But if all you're doing is going through

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:13.240
 and you want to touch in order each of the characters

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:16.700
 of the string, you then simply write a for loop,

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:17.920
 because it's more elegant.

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:21.760
 The less code you write, the less chance there is

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:23.040
 for you to make a mistake.

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:25.200
 And so the fact that these are equivalent,

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:27.480
 this is three lines of, well, two lines of a loop,

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:28.800
 and this is four lines of a loop,

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:31.700
 that's twice as many places as you could make a mistake,

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:34.520
 because you might, you know, misspell index or something.

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:37.020
 I mean, why even make an iteration variable

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:40.620
 if you don't need to make an iteration variable?

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:44.000
 And so we can do things that harken back

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:46.520
 to our iterations and loops chapter,

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:48.860
 where anything that you can do in those things,

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:50.320
 like look for the largest letter,

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:51.880
 look for the smallest letter,

00:07:51.880 --> 00:07:53.620
 search to see if a letter exists,

00:07:53.620 --> 00:07:57.640
 or say count the number of A's in the word banana.

00:07:57.640 --> 00:08:00.040
 And so that's what this is doing.

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:03.200
 And so we have a counter.

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:05.640
 So again, we do something at the top of the loop,

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:07.000
 we're going to do something in the middle loop,

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:08.160
 and then we're going to print it out at the bottom.

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:09.760
 So we start our counter at zero,

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:12.680
 we're going to loop through B, A, all the letters,

00:08:12.680 --> 00:08:14.280
 and then if the letter is A,

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:15.680
 then count equals count plus one.

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:18.020
 This is kind of a pattern in a loop

00:08:18.020 --> 00:08:19.740
 where we're noticing something,

00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:21.420
 and instead of like we did it earlier

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:23.160
 where we said found equals true,

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:24.420
 well, we're going to count 'em this time.

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:26.000
 So if we have one, we'll get one,

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:27.400
 if we have zero, we get zero,

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:31.360
 and how many there are, but there should be three,

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:33.280
 because it's going to run three times,

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:35.560
 and there's three A's in banana.

00:08:35.560 --> 00:08:39.800
 And so this is a conditional within count.

00:08:39.800 --> 00:08:42.940
 We've seen counts, we've seen conditionals in loop,

00:08:42.940 --> 00:08:44.440
 in prior chapters.

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:49.440
 And so again, I love the N keyword in Python.

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:53.360
 It again reminds me of set notation in algebra.

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:55.160
 If you're a math whiz, if you're not,

00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:57.920
 don't worry about it, or maybe you will be a math whiz,

00:08:57.920 --> 00:08:59.400
 and you'll say, whoa, this set notation

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:04.140
 reminds me a lot of the N keyword in Python.

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:11.560
 So again, it's for iteration variable letter,

00:09:11.560 --> 00:09:14.120
 again, don't get stuck with letter,

00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:16.840
 I just happen to be using it here, in banana.

00:09:16.840 --> 00:09:21.840
 And that is for each character in the string banana,

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:25.680
 run this loop once, changing the variable letter

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:28.320
 to be the particular character that we're pointing at.

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:29.480
 And so it's taking care,

00:09:29.480 --> 00:09:32.560
 for is taking care of a lot for us, right?

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:35.160
 And so this is sort of this really smart for loop.

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:38.520
 The for loop is both deciding how many times

00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:41.000
 to run the loop, in this case six,

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:42.480
 and it's advancing the letter.

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:47.080
 So advance, print, decide whether you're done,

00:09:47.080 --> 00:09:50.520
 advance, print, decide whether you're done, advance, print.

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:52.720
 Decide whether you're done, advance, print.

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:54.400
 Decide whether you're done, advance, print.

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:56.040
 Decide whether you're done, advance, print.

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:58.040
 Decide whether you're done, I am now done,

00:09:58.040 --> 00:10:00.840
 because I, whoop, you know,

00:10:00.840 --> 00:10:02.840
 we're done with that particular string.

00:10:02.840 --> 00:10:06.880
 And so you can think of the for as,

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:10.160
 you know, magically doing all of this for you,

00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:12.760
 of both deciding how long to run the loop,

00:10:12.760 --> 00:10:14.040
 when you're done or not,

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:18.120
 and moving down through all the successive letters

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:19.160
 in the loop.

00:10:19.160 --> 00:10:21.700
 So up next we'll do, talk a little bit about

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:24.000
 additional things that we can do with strings.

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:27.840
 (piano music)

00:10:32.840 --> 00:10:35.340
 (piano music)

